 ## Overview
-Iterative Bisection Intersection Refinement is an iterative root-finding method used to refine the intersection position after a sign change has been detected during ray marching [S1]. It narrows down the interval $[t_{prev}, t_{curr}]$ containing the isosurface by iteratively applying linear interpolation (bisection) within the segment [S2]. This approach solves the problem of geometric inaccuracy inherent in discrete sampling, providing a balance between computational speed and visual fidelity [S2, S3]. Unlike closed-form algebraic solutions or higher-order root isolation techniques, it relies on repeated linear approximation [S1]. It builds on [Linear Edge Intersection](868-linear-edge-intersection.md) by applying similar linear interpolation principles within an iterative refinement loop over a ray segment rather than a single edge [S2].
